WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.319
Three aquifer models.

2
00:00:01.319 --> 00:00:02.637
Three different mechanisms.

3
00:00:02.637 --> 00:00:08.352
Yet under a pumping test, they can cast almost the same drawdown curve.

4
00:00:08.792 --> 00:00:14.516
In the ideal classical baseline, diffusion supplies one effective time scale.

5
00:00:14.516 --> 00:00:16.597
Transmissivity spreads the signal.

6
00:00:16.597 --> 00:00:20.240
Storage sets how much water is released.

7
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:29.556
But preferential pathways, dual porosity exchange, or coupled processes can make flux and head equilibrate on different time scales.

8
00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:35.101
In data, those different stories may collapse into one observable memory band.

9
00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:42.332
Lagging Theory is one low order way to represent that band, not proof of a unique mechanism.

10
00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:51.620
With adequate bandwidth and signal quality, a frequency sweep can test how many stable dynamic modes repeat across periods and wells.

11
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.608
If only one extra band survives, a single memory model may be enough.

12
00:00:57.608 --> 00:01:04.388
A second band should earn its place by reappearing beyond noise, sampling limits, and model compensation.

13
00:01:04.888 --> 00:01:12.736
So should aquifer models be classified by the mechanisms we can imagine, or by the memories the data can actually reveal?
